  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/assets/3c5450b18c5dc58b082faadf4086b6a21c0c9b55afb016090147609a2ccfe719.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}\u20613\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}\u20613.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}\u20613.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int [-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}\u20613]\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={3}^{1-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2} & ={3}^{1-2}+C \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-1}+\\frac{11}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{28}{3}} \\\\  & \u2248-3.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
